WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.250
Corey Mandell: The story of the manager was,
I was in a writing group and there were some

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:05.260
professional writers.

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:07.030
There was actually an Academy Award nominated
writer.

00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:09.269
We met at her house (that was really exciting).

00:00:09.269 --> 00:00:13.039
So I had written a script and they were giving
me feedback and long story short, it got to

00:00:13.039 --> 00:00:15.679
the point where they said “This script is
amazing.

00:00:15.679 --> 00:00:16.679
This script will sell!

00:00:16.679 --> 00:00:18.560
The only question is how much will it sell
for?

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:20.730
Do you want help getting an agent?”

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:24.040
And of course that was like magic to my ears.

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:29.130
And then I mentioned to my professor at UCLA
who you know a lot of really big writers had

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:31.630
come through that school and his class.

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:35.770
So he volunteered to read the script and he
said “This is one of the three best scripts

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:37.680
I’ve ever read in 20 years at UCLA.”

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.930
Again, I’m thinking of all these amazing
writers and so I’m thinking “Well I guess

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:42.960
I’m as good as those!”

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.410
And he goes “Well this will definitely sell.

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:47.670
Do you want help getting and manager or agent?”

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:52.550
I was interning for a manager at the time,
as an intern.

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:55.730
As a favor to that manager I’m like “Do
you want to read this?

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:58.399
It’s going to sell.

00:00:58.399 --> 00:01:01.410
Do you want your 10% or not buddy?”

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:05.019
He agreed to read it (it took like 3 weeks,
which drove me crazy).

00:01:05.019 --> 00:01:07.040
It was a meeting that I’ll never forget.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.720
I go in there and he says “This is a pretty
good rough draft.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:12.610
Don’t show it to anyone in the industry.

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:16.979
Because when you show stuff to people in the
industry, they get coverage reports on it

00:01:16.979 --> 00:01:17.980
and those are databased.

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:19.540
That’s your first impression.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:23.950
But if it’s not just great coverage, you
haven’t just blown your chance with the

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:27.320
production company, they are going to share
that coverage with everybody in the database.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:31.369
So you’ve blown your first impression across
the board.

00:01:31.369 --> 00:01:33.920
And he said “This will not serve you well.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:35.479
But…you have some real strengths.

00:01:35.479 --> 00:01:36.640
You have some real weaknesses.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:41.200
You have some blindspots.I can help you with
that,” and he’s giving me this whole talk.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.180
And the whole time I’m like “What is wrong
with him?”

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:47.010
I was like “Did I tell you what the Academy
Award nominated person said?

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:53.000
Did I say that my professor who taught Steven….you
know.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:57.600
And he said “Look, they were all telling
you the truth.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.100
This is a better script than most scripts.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.100
It’s an impressive script.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:04.869
But it’s not nearly as good as what you
need in the industry.

00:02:04.869 --> 00:02:06.540
People in the writing group are working writers.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:10.110
The professor at UCLA…all smart people.

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:12.989
But they are not in the business of breaking
writers into the business.

00:02:12.989 --> 00:02:14.030
I am.

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:16.190
I know how good something has to be.

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:18.610
This isn’t close.”

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:20.730
I didn’t want to believe that.

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:24.180
My fixed mindset is like “He’s an idiot.”

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:30.930
So what he said and this was the best thing
anybody could have said was “Hire three

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:32.690
studio readers.

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:34.500
Pay them under the table.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:38.790
If this script came through the tracking system,
what coverage would you write and then give

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:40.190
me the coverage.

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:41.190
Honest coverage.

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:44.290
And if the coverage isn’t good, it’s not
going to hurt you.

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:45.300
Just you only see it.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:46.300
No one will see it.”

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:49.401
I didn’t have a lot of money, but I did
this.

00:02:49.401 --> 00:02:55.140
I paid like $100-$150 bucks to someone at
Imagine Entertainment, someone at Warner Brothers

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:57.720
and someone at Scott Rudin’s company.

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:01.510
The coverage came back and it wasn’t terrible.

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:03.350
It wasn’t bad.

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:06.900
It was okay (which is terrible).

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:09.590
It would have hurt me.

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:12.900
It was clear that this coverage was “This
is okay.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:13.910
Nothing special.

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:15.750
Nothing great here.

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:20.599
And then on the writer it said “Recommend,
consider.”

00:03:20.599 --> 00:03:25.409
It was low marks on the writing.

00:03:25.409 --> 00:03:27.660
And you can’t hide from that.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:32.440
The manager said “I’ll work with you if
you’re willing to put your ego at the door

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:34.100
and do this training.”

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:36.519
I did and it was like 14 months.

00:03:36.519 --> 00:03:37.790
It was grueling.

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:40.920
He was…he was not a good person.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:43.129
But his training was good.

00:03:43.129 --> 00:03:44.960
It was much rougher than it had to be but
okay.

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:49.940
And at the end of that time he said “I now
think the script is where it needs to be.

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:51.819
I’ll pay for coverage.”

00:03:51.819 --> 00:03:55.959
And he paid for three coverage reports with
three different people (real actual readers).

00:03:55.959 --> 00:04:01.180
And it was all just exactly what he wanted
(exceptional coverage).

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:05.240
Literally three weeks later Ridley Scott hires
me to write METROPOLIS.

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:07.270
He flies me to London.

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:09.400
Literally my whole world changed.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:11.080
My whole life changed.

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:19.120
And it never would have happened if that manager
hadn’t sort of forced me to pay for coverage.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:21.069
Because at some point the fixed mindset can’t
hide.

00:04:21.069 --> 00:04:24.689
That’s what I always tell people.

00:04:24.689 --> 00:04:27.990
The biggest mistake you can make is to go
out to marketplace before you’re ready.

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:29.710
Just because your friends think it’s great.

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:31.469
Just because your teacher thinks it’s great.

00:04:31.469 --> 00:04:35.050
Just because the script consultant you hired
says it’s great…and I don’t do it anymore

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:39.750
(I used to do script consulting) but I say
don’t listen to those people because they

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:44.789
often…they are running a business and a
business wants happy, repeat customers, so

00:04:44.789 --> 00:04:46.430
they will always tell you what you want to
hear.

00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:50.279
And even if you find a script consultant which
has incredible integrity (which is rare but

00:04:50.279 --> 00:04:54.219
does exists), they are not in the business
of breaking writers into the business.

00:04:54.219 --> 00:04:57.219
And I wouldn’t listen to their advice, per
se.

00:04:57.219 --> 00:05:00.909
I know people who are like “The script consultant
said this, this and this.

00:05:00.909 --> 00:05:03.710
I went out to the marketplace, look at the
coverage, it stunk!”

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:04.930
It happens all the time.

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:11.319
I say hire actual readers under the table
in TV or feature or wherever you are and get

00:05:11.319 --> 00:05:13.900
the actual coverage report that they would
get.

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:16.159
If all three coverage reports come back “Recommend!

00:05:16.159 --> 00:05:17.159
Recommend!

00:05:17.159 --> 00:05:18.379
Highest marks!”

00:05:18.379 --> 00:05:23.129
Then you’re ready to go out…highest mountain…pound
your chest and get everybody to read your

00:05:23.129 --> 00:05:24.129
script.

00:05:24.129 --> 00:05:25.990
If that’s not the case, stay off the radar.

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:29.240
You don’t want people reading material if
it’s going to hurt you.

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:32.409
And figure out how to train yourself to get
better.

00:05:32.409 --> 00:05:38.229
So (sorry for a long answer) but no, I was
very fixed mindset and if that manager hadn’t

00:05:38.229 --> 00:05:41.219
done that for me, I’d be taking classes
today.

00:05:41.219 --> 00:05:42.169
I wouldn’t be teaching them.

